    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Earthquakes can rupture across steps between strike-slip faults to host earthquakes larger than those predicted from individual fault lengths. We study the influence of non-planar fault geometry, remote driving stress, and friction on the potential failure of faults through dilational steps. The perturbed stress fields resolved on planar, vertical faults due to slip along a planar and non-planar fault have similar potential step widths. For changes in friction, potential step width increases non-linearly with mean slip for both fault geometries. Potential step width increases non-linearly as mean slip increases along the planar fault due to changes in the remote stress orientation, but for the non-planar fault, similar increases in potential step width due to changes in the remote stress orientation are independent of mean slip.\r\n\r\nUsing non-planar receiver fault surfaces that better approximate known faults, we find that, in the 1992 M 7.3 Landers, California earthquake, slip transfer across the dilational step between the Johnson Valley and Homestead Valley faults is more likely with the contribution of quasi-static stress from slip along the Landers-Kickapoo Fault, located within the step. This is consistent for orientations of the maximum remote compressive stress from 011°-021° and for friction coefficients from 0.50-0.70. The role of the LKF is more ambiguous with the orientation of the maximum remote compressive stress below 011° and with a more vertical fault structure, highlighting the influence of remote stress orientation and fault geometry on multifault mechanics. Failure of the Homestead Valley Fault inhibits failure along the northern Johnson Valley Fault, emphasizing that fault interactions can discourage as well as encourage fault slip across a step.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
